problem_statement.body:
## Summary

GLSL, HLSL and CrossGL convert an `int` operand to `uint` when the other operand of a comparison or arithmetic operator is a `uint` (GLSL 4.60 section 4.1.10). WGSL has no such conversion, and the WGSL generator writes both operands as they are, so naga rejects the module. The commonest case is a `uint` loop index bounded by an array or buffer `length()`, which is an `int`.

## Reproducer

```glsl
shader Probe {
    buffer float* values;
    buffer float* outp;
    compute {
        layout(local_size_x = 1, local_size_y = 1, local_size_z = 1) in;
        void main() {
            uint i = 2u;
            int k = 3;
            if (i < k) {
                outp[0] = 1.0;
            }
            for (uint j = 0u; j < values.length(); j++) {
                outp[j] = values[j];
            }
        }
    }
}
```

```bash
python -m crosstl translate probe.cgl --backend wgsl --output -
```

## Actual (main at 9ccff053)

```wgsl
    if ((i < k))     {
    ...
    for (var j: u32 = 0u; (j < i32(arrayLength(&values))); j += 1)     {
```

naga 30.0.1:

```
error: Entry point compute_main at Compute is invalid
13 │     if ((i < k))     {
   = Operation Less can't work with [4] (of type Scalar(Scalar { kind: Uint, width: 4 })) and [5] (of type Scalar(Scalar { kind: Sint, width: 4 }))
```

The loop condition alone fails the same way, as do `j < values.length()` for `buffer float values[]` and `buffer float values[4]` (`(j < i32(4))`), and the `StructuredBuffer<T>` form. Arithmetic and built-in arguments have the same gap: `uint last = values.length() - 1u;` becomes `var last: u32 = (i32(arrayLength(&values)) - 1u);` ("the type of `last` is expected to be `u32`, but got `i32`"), and `uint n = min(values.length(), 4u);` becomes `min(i32(arrayLength(&values)), 4u)` ("inconsistent type passed as argument #2 to `min`"). GLSL output already converts the comparison: `if ((i < uint(k)))`.

## Expected

When one operand of a comparison or arithmetic operator (or of `min`, `max` and `clamp`) is `u32` and the other is a typed `i32`, WGSL output converts the `i32` one: `(i < u32(k))`, `(j < u32(i32(arrayLength(&values))))` and `(u32(i32(arrayLength(&values))) - 1u)`. Integer literals are abstract in WGSL and need no conversion.

#3213 is the same gap for `int` and `uint` operands next to a `float`. #3617 fixed `.length()` where a `uint` is expected (declarations, assignments, arguments and returns).

Found while reviewing #3620.

